KT412 (Dongwon DW02-412H) — ath9k 2.4G FINAL hardware power register floor.

This is the third leg of the 30 dBm extreme build, applied to the mac80211
*backports* ath9k source (package/kernel/mac80211/patches/ath9k/) — the tree
that actually compiles into kmod-ath9k. Patching the in-tree kernel does nothing.

990 lifts the EEPROM per-rate targets (targetPowerValT2 => max_power_level =>
iw phy / iwinfo / netdev display). 991 pins the cfg80211 regulatory ceiling so
handle_channel() does not re-clamp 2.4G back to 24. This patch (992) drives the
FINAL value that ath9k_hw_apply_txpower() hands to eep_ops->set_txpower() — i.e.
the number programmed into the hardware power register — to >= 60 half-dB
(30.0 dBm) on the 2.4 GHz band, so the chip itself is pushed to 30 (maximal
Level A; "as hard as stock allows"). Capped at MAX_COMBINED_POWER.

Minimal hunk: only the most stable context lines are used (the new_pwr min_t
line above, and the blank line + set_txpower call below) so quilt's context
match survives backports line-number drift at this function.

Marker (post-compile, dmesg at radio bring-up): KT412-30DBM-FINAL-NETDEV-POWER.

ART/EEPROM/caldata are NEVER written. Conducted RF above the PA saturation is
clipped; the register reads 30 but radiated power does not exceed PA physics.

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath9k/hw.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath9k/hw.c
@@ -2999,4 +2999,10 @@
 	new_pwr = min_t(int, chan_pwr, reg->power_limit);

+	if (channel->band == NL80211_BAND_2GHZ) {
+		new_pwr = max_t(int, new_pwr, 60);
+		new_pwr = min_t(int, new_pwr, MAX_COMBINED_POWER);
+		printk_once(KERN_INFO "KT412-30DBM-FINAL-NETDEV-POWER\n");
+	}
+
 	ah->eep_ops->set_txpower(ah, chan, ctl,
 				 get_antenna_gain(ah, chan), new_pwr, test);
